		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=39337#p39337"><![CDATA[
Hi Adrian,<br><br>I haven't checked your code so I cannot tell whether there is a bug in there. I do know that due to rounding issues it can happen that no proper simplex is found. In fact, the original paper by Gilbert Johnson and Keerthi already mentions the issue and offers a solution in form of backup procedure. The backup procedure computes the closest point for each sub-simplex and simply keeps the closest one. This procedure is more expensive than the normal Johnson procedure but bullet proof (no pun). After a call to the backup procedure the GJK loop is terminated as the current simplex is probably too flat to give you proper results. Others ways to handle rounding noise are:<br><br>(a) Check whether a support point has already been returned earlier. This would work only for pairs of polytopes. (Super)quadrics have infinite support points and are likely to never return the same one.<br>(b) Check whether the new closet point is significantly closer to the origin than the one from previous iteration. If the difference in distance is less than some epsilon times the current distance then you have run into numerical problems. Return the point from previous iteration as the current one is probably too noisy.<br><br>Cheers,<br><br>Gino<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=4">gino</a> — Sun May 07, 2017 7:56 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
